Setting. Earlier pages study P^1(C) minus {0,1,infinity}, call it U. Its automorphisms, holomorphic and antiholomorphic, form Gamma = S3 x Z/2. S3 is generated by the involutions sigma_0, sigma_1, sigma_infinity (for example sigma_1(z) = 1/z). The Z/2 is generated by complex conjugation tau. Sigma is the group of all lifts of Gamma to the universal cover. It is an extension
1 -> pi_1(U) = <l_0, l_1, l_inf | l_inf l_1 l_0 = 1> (free of rank 2) -> Sigma -> S3 x Z/2 -> 1.
The stated aim (struck-through opening, continued from p.28) is a presentation of Sigma by generators and relations. The method: start from a presentation of Gamma, lift the generators judiciously, express l_0, l_1, l_inf as words in the lifts, and add the relation l_inf l_1 l_0 = 1.

Presentation of Gamma: sigma_i^2 = 1; sigma_0 sigma_1 = sigma_1 sigma_inf = sigma_inf sigma_0 = rho; rho^3 = 1; tau^2 = 1; tau central.

The lifts are:
- involutions sigma_0, sigma_1, sigma_inf (each sigma_i has fixed points in U, so it lifts to an elliptic element of order 2);
- rho of order 3;
- three lifts tau_0, tau_1, tau_inf of tau. Tau fixes the three real arcs of U, so each lift is the hyperbolic reflection in one side of the ideal triangle with vertices 0, 1, infinity.

Relations written on the page:
- all sigma_i^2 = tau_i^2 = 1;
- rho^3 = 1;
- rho permutes the tau_i cyclically;
- tau_i commutes with sigma_i;
- sigma_0 sigma_1 = tau_inf tau_1 rho, and its cyclic analogues.
The margin adds that rho also permutes the sigma_i cyclically.

The boxed NB identifies the loops as products of reflections in adjacent sides: l_0 = tau_inf tau_1, l_1 = tau_0 tau_inf, l_inf = tau_1 tau_0. Each is the parabolic fixing the corresponding cusp. Then l_inf l_1 l_0 = 1 holds automatically. Hence <tau_0, tau_1, tau_inf> is the reflection group of the ideal triangle, Z/2*Z/2*Z/2. It contains pi_1(U) (identified with the image of Gamma(2)) with index 2.

The struck question (can rho be eliminated?) is settled on p.14 differently: keep rho, sigma_0, tau_0 as generators. This gives Sigma = ((Z/2 x Z/2) * Z/3) / << [rho, sigma_0][rho^{-1}, tau_0] >>, and Grothendieck observes an automorphism swapping sigma_0 with tau_0 and rho with rho^{-1}.

In modern terms Sigma is the extended modular group PGL2(Z) = PSL2(Z) ⋊ <z -> -z bar>. Equivalently it is the extended (2,3,infinity) triangle group. The quotient of PGL2(Z) by the image of Gamma(2) is the full automorphism group S3 x Z/2 of the lambda-line. The page is a hands-on derivation of a presentation of PGL2(Z) adapted to the Legendre-lambda / Gamma(2) picture. It is part of the 'Printemps 81' study of the action of the absolute Galois group, including complex conjugation, on pi_1 of P^1 minus three points and its symmetric quotients.
